Reading through your code, there doesn't seem to be a corresponding comment starting with "@@@". As such, I can't produce any output assertions for this particular code. If you are seeking to create assertion statements similar to Debug.Assert() in C#, you may want to review your comments and ensure they contain the text "@@@" at the start. You can use the ReFind in Notepad++ (or similar tool) to highlight all instances of the comment to review. 

If you mean to assert a certain condition after every method, this would typically be handled by the Testing Framework. The specifics would depend on your testing framework, but generally this involves creating a Unit Test and then asserting an expected result.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(index <= New_arrListLastIndexOf_x);
```--------------------Code to generate output assert statements:
```cs
            // @@@ index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_index, "Error: index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array.");
```

In the code above, the second argument is a message that will be shown if the condition is false. If an index passed in is less than or equal to the last index of a specific value in the new arrayed list, it will pass the test. If this condition is met, no error message will be output, and the if this condition is not met, an error message "Error: index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array." will output in debugging.--------------------I'm unable to run the code, but if your comments start with "@@@" and followed by some instructions, then your comments probably need to be translated into assertion statements. Note that you may need to adjust the generated Assert based on the specific requirements.

Please take a look at the following code where I assume that the index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array:
```csharp
        // @@@ index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array 
        Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOf_x >= Old_index, "index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array");
```
This debug assert statement checks if New_arrListLastIndexOf_x is greater than or equal to Old_index. If it's not, the assert will fail and indicate that the "index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array" is not true. 

Also, please remove the `PexAssumeUnderTest` attribute which is unnecessary and might be causing issues.

Remember, these are assertion statements and they're typically used for testing a particular condition. In this case, we're checking if the IndexOf x in the new array list is greater or equal to that which was being used earlier. If they're not equal, then there could be an error in your code, or perhaps the index provided is out of bounds or invalid.--------------------The code can be modified to show outputs as follows.

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList[index] = x;

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= index);
            
	    // Outputs will be displayed as part of the test method execution, not as comments
	}
    }
}
```
This should generate a debug assertion at each step when running your test as long as the condition is not met.--------------------The csharp code you've mentioned uses PEX framework for generating test cases which generates different test cases based on assertions and assumptions embedded in them.

In response to your comment "@@@ index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array", following Debug.Assert statement can be used:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX, "index passed in is greater than last index of x in new array");
```

This Assert statement will check if the the new index 'New_arrListIndexOfX' is less than or equal to the last index 'New_arrListLastIndexOfX' of x in the new array. If the index 'New_arrListIndexOfX' is greater than 'New_arrListLastIndexOfX', then the test fails and indicates potential problem in the method. 

As per Debugger, it will throw an exception if the condition fails. In unit test cases, the Assert statement throws an exception when the condition fails and allows the rest of the code to continue.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1, "Array list count doesn't match");
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_index, "Index of x is incorrect");
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1, "Last index of x is incorrect");
Debug.Assert(!New_arrListContainsX || New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX, "Does not check the array list contains x");
```
These assertions check that the array count has increased by one, the new array list contains the element at the index passed in, and that the last index of x is updated correctly. It also makes sure that the array list contains x property is updated correctly. If all values or most of them are updated correctly, the array list operations are correctly written and the code is sound.--------------------Here are the "Debug.Assert" statements corresponding to your comments:

```cs
Debug.Assert(this.index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);

Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
```

These statements can be added in lines after the given statement that require an assertion. Note that you should surround these statements with a try-catch block to capture any exceptions that might be thrown.--------------------Here are the debug assert statements corresponding to the comments in the given csharp code. Please note that these assertions will be true if the "index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array" condition is met. Otherwise, the assertions will fail and you will get detailed information about what values caused the failure.

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
```

Note: Remember that these assertion checks are for your test to work, not a form of "guarantee" for your code. Running your code with assertions turned off (Debug->Windows->Diagnostic Tools) can make the messages less likely to appear, and they're not meant to be conditions under which your code will always function as expected. They're a tool for diagnosing problems in your test data or in your program's design.--------------------Here is the corresponding C# code for outputting Assert statements according to the comments that start with "@@@":

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array");
```

This Assert statement will ensure that the index passed in is less than or equal to the last occurrence of the old element (`x`) in the updated (`new`) array (`arrList`).--------------------